# WHITELIST, NOT A BLOCKLIST, BECAUSE THIS REPO IS PUBLIC.
#
# A pattern list enumerates the bad and is never finished: it catches the secret
# you thought of and not the one inline in a script. Where the consequence of a
# mistake is PUBLICATION, enumerate the good instead. Ignore everything, then
# allow only what belongs.
#
# Proved both ways on 2026-10-05, not just one:
#   negative control  a planted fake key file is NOT staged by add -A
#   positive control  a new legitimate file in an allowed path IS staged
# A whitelist that stages nothing would pass the first test and break the repo.
#
# ⚠️ THE COST, STATED: a new file in a path not listed below is silently NOT
# committed. That is the trade, and it is the right way round for a public repo.
# If something you added does not appear in git status, this file is why.

# 1. Ignore everything.
*

# 2. But descend into directories, or nothing inside them can be matched.
!*/

# 3. Allow what belongs.
!.gitignore
!LICENSE
!README.md
!ARTICLE.md
!ARTICLE-for-syndication.md
!DEAD-TABLES.md
!tenant_isolation_audit.sql
!check-verdicts.sh
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!server.json
!mcp/README.md
!mcp/test.js
!mcp/src/*.js
!site/*.html
!site/.vercelignore

# 4. And never these, even inside an allowed directory.
node_modules/
site/.vercel/
.env
.env.*
